### Day 25 Lab — Complete AgentCore + OBO Flow
### This HTTP request file traces all 8 steps of the AgentCore → IS 7.3 → Payment API flow

# ============================================================================
# STEP 1: User authenticates to IS 7.3
# ============================================================================
# User uses App-Native Auth or browser OIDC to get an IS 7.3 access token

# Request (browser-based, not shown in HTTP format, but the result is:)

# Response from IS 7.3:
# HTTP 200 OK
# {
#   "access_token": "eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...",
#   "token_type": "Bearer",
#   "expires_in": 3600,
#   "scope": "payments:initiate"
# }
#
# JWT payload (decoded):
# {
#   "iss": "https://is.bank.com:9443/oauth2/token",
#   "sub": "user-123",
#   "aud": "banking-app",
#   "scope": "payments:initiate",
#   "exp": 1727786400,
#   "iat": 1727782800,
#   "jti": "uuid-user-token-jti"
# }

### Step 1 - User token obtained
@USER_ACCESS_TOKEN = eyJ...  # <PLACEHOLDER: full user access token from IS 7.3>
@USER_ID = user-123

# ============================================================================
# STEP 2: User token passed to AgentCore session context (out-of-band)
# ============================================================================
# This happens via secure app-level mechanism (not HTTP).
# User's app calls AgentCore API or stores token in secure session store.

# ============================================================================
# STEP 3: AgentCore obtains OIDC token from AWS STS
# ============================================================================
# AgentCore has already assumed an IAM role with session tags.
# AgentCore now calls AWS STS to get an OIDC identity token.
# The agent can make this call because it has temporary IAM credentials.

# This is NOT an HTTP request we show here (it's AWS STS internal).
# But the result is an OIDC token signed by AWS:
#
# {
#   "iss": "https://oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/EXAMPLEBUCKET",
#   "sub": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/PaymentOrchestratorAgentRole",
#   "aud": "https://is.bank.com:9443/oauth2/token",
#   "exp": 1727786400,
#   "iat": 1727782800,
#   "kid": "example-key-id"
# }
# Signature: signed by AWS private key, verifiable via AWS JWKS

### Step 3 - OIDC token obtained
@AGENT_OIDC_TOKEN = eyJ...  # <PLACEHOLDER: AWS OIDC token>
@AGENT_ROLE_ARN = arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/PaymentOrchestratorAgentRole

# ============================================================================
# STEP 4: IS 7.3 External IdP Registration (one-time setup)
# ============================================================================
# IS 7.3 must be configured to trust AWS OIDC tokens.
# This is typically done in Console, but shown as HTTP here for reference.

POST https://is.bank.com:9443/api/identity/providers HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer <PLACEHOLDER: admin-token>

{
  "provider_name": "AWS-OIDC",
  "provider_type": "OpenIDConnect",
  "enabled": true,
  "discovery_url": "https://oidc.eks.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/id/<PLACEHOLDER: cluster-id>/.well-known/openid-configuration",
  "client_id": "is-bank-idp-client",
  "client_secret": "<PLACEHOLDER: secret>",
  "scopes": ["openid", "profile"],
  "name_id_format": "http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/nameidentifier"
}

# Response:
# HTTP 201 Created
# {
#   "provider_id": "aws-oidc-idp-001",
#   "status": "active"
# }

# ============================================================================
# STEP 5 & 6: Agent calls IS 7.3 token exchange endpoint
# ============================================================================

POST https://is.bank.com:9443/oauth2/token HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Host: is.bank.com:9443

grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange
&subject_token={{USER_ACCESS_TOKEN}}
&subject_token_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:access_token
&actor_token={{AGENT_OIDC_TOKEN}}
&actor_token_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt
&scope=payments:initiate
&requested_token_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:access_token
&client_assertion_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:client-assertion-type:jwt-bearer
&client_assertion=<PLACEHOLDER: agent-private-key-jwt>

# Notes on this request:
# - subject_token: User's IS 7.3 token (obtained in Step 1)
# - actor_token: Agent's AWS OIDC token (obtained in Step 3)
# - scope: Must be a subset of subject_token's scope
# - client_assertion: Agent's private_key_jwt for authentication to IS 7.3
#
# IS 7.3 processing (Steps 5 in the flow):
#   1. Validate client_assertion (agent's private_key_jwt) → authenticates agent to IS 7.3
#   2. Validate subject_token signature → confirms it's a valid IS 7.3 token
#   3. Extract sub=user-123 from subject_token
#   4. Fetch AWS OIDC JWKS from discovery_url (cached)
#   5. Validate actor_token signature using AWS public key
#   6. Check actor_token.aud == "https://is.bank.com:9443/oauth2/token"
#   7. Extract sub=agent-role from actor_token
#   8. Check actor trust policy: is agent-role allowed to exchange for user-123?
#   9. If all checks pass, issue exchange token

### Step 5 & 6 - Token Exchange
@IS73_TOKEN_ENDPOINT = https://is.bank.com:9443/oauth2/token

# ============================================================================
# RESPONSE: Exchange Token (Step 6)
# ============================================================================

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "access_token": "eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...",
  "token_type": "Bearer",
  "expires_in": 900,
  "scope": "payments:initiate",
  "issued_token_type": "urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:access_token"
}

# Decoded JWT payload:
# {
#   "iss": "https://is.bank.com:9443/oauth2/token",
#   "sub": "user-123",
#   "act": {
#     "sub": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/PaymentOrchestratorAgentRole"
#   },
#   "aud": "payment-api",
#   "scope": "payments:initiate",
#   "azp": "payment-orchestrator-client",
#   "exp": 1727783700,
#   "iat": 1727782800,
#   "jti": "uuid-exchange-token-jti"
# }

# Key observations:
# - sub: PRESERVED from subject_token (original user)
# - act.sub: ADDED from actor_token (agent's identity)
# - scope: NARROWED to what was requested (payments:initiate)
# - exp: SHORTER TTL (15 minutes = 900 seconds) vs user token (1 hour)
# - aud: Token is for payment-api

@EXCHANGE_TOKEN = eyJ...  # <PLACEHOLDER: exchange token from response>

# ============================================================================
# STEP 7: Agent calls Payment API with exchange token
# ============================================================================

POST https://payment-api.bank.com/api/payments/initiate HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{EXCHANGE_TOKEN}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": 100.00,
  "currency": "USD",
  "recipient": "account-456",
  "reference": "Payment from agent on behalf of user"
}

# Notes:
# - Bearer token is the exchange token from Step 6
# - Token has sub=user-123, act.sub=agent, scope=payments:initiate
# - Payment API can trust this token because:
#   1. IS 7.3 signed it (verified by Payment API via JWKS)
#   2. IS 7.3 validated the full chain (user token + agent OIDC token)

# ============================================================================
# STEP 8: Payment API introspects the token before processing
# ============================================================================

POST https://is.bank.com:9443/oauth2/introspect HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Authorization: Basic <PLACEHOLDER: payment-api-credentials>

token={{EXCHANGE_TOKEN}}
&token_type_hint=access_token

# Response:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "active": true,
  "scope": "payments:initiate",
  "client_id": "payment-orchestrator-client",
  "username": "user-123",
  "sub": "user-123",
  "act": {
    "sub": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/PaymentOrchestratorAgentRole"
  },
  "aud": "payment-api",
  "iss": "https://is.bank.com:9443/oauth2/token",
  "exp": 1727783700,
  "iat": 1727782800,
  "jti": "uuid-exchange-token-jti"
}

# Key observations:
# - active=true: Token is valid and not revoked
# - sub=user-123: Original user (can scope data access to this user)
# - act.sub=agent-role: Acting agent (can enforce agent-specific policies)
# - scope=payments:initiate: Requested operation is within scope
# - aud=payment-api: Token was issued for this API

# Payment API decision logic:
# ✅ active=true → Token is valid
# ✅ scope contains "payments:initiate" → Operation is authorized
# ✅ sub=user-123 → Scope API access to this user's records
# ✅ act.sub matches orchestrator role → Log this action as orchestrator agent

# ============================================================================
# CONTINUATION: Payment API processes the request
# ============================================================================

# Response from Payment API:
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "transaction_id": "txn-20261001-12345",
  "status": "initiated",
  "amount": 100.00,
  "currency": "USD",
  "recipient": "account-456",
  "timestamp": "2026-10-01T14:33:45Z",
  "initiated_by": {
    "user": "user-123",
    "agent": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/PaymentOrchestratorAgentRole",
    "session_id": "<from AgentCore caller_identity>"
  }
}

# ============================================================================
# SCENARIO: User revokes consent — token becomes invalid
# ============================================================================
# User opens IS 7.3 Console and clicks "Revoke" on the payment orchestrator agent.

# Revocation takes effect immediately in IS 7.3:
# IS 7.3 marks: user-123's consent for payment-orchestrator = REVOKED

# Agent attempts another payment API call (still has exchange token):

POST https://payment-api.bank.com/api/payments/initiate HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Bearer {{EXCHANGE_TOKEN}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "amount": 200.00,
  "currency": "USD",
  "recipient": "account-789"
}

# Payment API introspects again:

POST https://is.bank.com:9443/oauth2/introspect HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Authorization: Basic <PLACEHOLDER: payment-api-credentials>

token={{EXCHANGE_TOKEN}}

# Response: Token is now REVOKED

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "active": false,
  "error": "revoked",
  "error_description": "Token has been revoked by user consent withdrawal"
}

# Payment API sees active=false and rejects:

HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "error": "invalid_token",
  "error_description": "Token is revoked or expired"
}

# ============================================================================
# IMPORTANT NOTES
# ============================================================================
#
# 1. SCOPE NARROWING:
#    If subject_token has scope="payments:read payments:write accounts:read"
#    and agent requests scope="payments:initiate"
#    Then IS 7.3 issues token with scope="payments:initiate" (narrowed)
#    Agent CANNOT use the same token for accounts:read
#
# 2. ACTOR TRUST POLICY:
#    IS 7.3 must have actor trust policy configured (per-application in Console):
#    - PaymentOrchestratorRole: allowed
#    - FraudCheckerToolRole: allowed
#    - AdminProvisioningRole: NOT allowed
#    If AdminProvisioningRole tries to exchange, IS 7.3 returns:
#    HTTP 400 Bad Request
#    {
#      "error": "invalid_grant",
#      "error_description": "actor_not_trusted_for_exchange"
#    }
#
# 3. INTROSPECTION CACHING:
#    Payment API may cache introspection results (typical cache TTL: 5 minutes)
#    If user revokes and Payment API has a cached "active=true" response,
#    it may take up to 5 minutes for the revocation to propagate.
#    This is a compliance tradeoff: lower latency at cost of revocation delay.
#
# 4. REVOCATION PROPAGATION:
#    When user token is revoked or expires:
#    - All derived exchange tokens become invalid on next introspection
#    - Already-cached introspection responses remain until cache expires
#    - APIs using local JWT validation (no introspection) will not see revocation
#    - Banking compliance mandates introspection-based validation
#
